      "commit_id": null,
      "commit_url": null,
      "created_at": "2026-10-29T15:31:50Z",
      "updated_at": "2026-10-29T15:31:50Z",
      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "body": "> the ctor should simply accept a `std::vector<T> vin/out`. This keeps all call sites clean and possibly also avoids a copy\n\n@maflcko, the problem is that moving a vector into place allows the following:\n\n```cpp\nstd::vector<CTxIn> inputs = ...;\nCTxIn& backdoor = inputs.back();\n\nconst CTransaction tx{std::move(inputs), ...};\n\nbackdoor = ...; // <- modify `tx`, potentially breaking its invariant\n```\n\nSo it is better to *not try to avoid the copy*. But if a copy needs to be made, why should we enforce the type of the temporary? The compiler cannot deduce template arguments from bare braced initializer lists, which I would not consider a big problem here. I think it actually helps readability to specify a type because it communicates to the reader: \"I am creating a container\" rather than \"I am creating some object\". Using `std::array` seems to have less overhead than `std::vector` here and is easier to type than `std::initializer_list<CTxIn>`, though all three would work in the same way.\n\nIf performance of the copy becomes a concern, we should strive for making construction `constexpr` (not proposed here).",
      "user": {
