      "created_at": "2026-10-30T15:56:35Z",
      "updated_at": "2026-10-30T15:56:35Z",
      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "body": "To summarise where the constructor discussion stands as I understand it: the aliasing concern can be addressed by either design, as noted above, so it does not decide between them. What remains:\n\n- **Literal lists:** explicit `std::array{...}` versus plain braces (with one element per line and trailing commas). The explicit container makes it clear that the inputs and outputs are each a collection, and where each one ends. Plain braces are less verbose, and since every element already names its type, the container name adds little. For an empty list, however, a `{}` doesn't say what it is, whereas `std::array<CTxIn, 0>{}` is unambiguous.\n\n- **Flexibility:** the range constructor accepts any range directly, including views of derived inputs. The vector constructor needs those converted first: through the iterator-pair constructor on a separate line in C++20, or inline with `| std::ranges::to<std::vector>()` in C++23. If it also copies to avoid aliasing, the elements are copied twice. For example, with the range constructor, a child spending output 0 of every parent (as in [`txpackage_tests`](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/blob/43a33e513c4044dc01d92e77da76f2e98faaedb5/src/test/txpackage_tests.cpp#L291-L301)) could be written as: \n\n  ```cpp\n  const CTransaction child{\n      package | std::views::transform([](const CTransactionRef& p) { \n          return CTxIn{COutPoint{p->GetHash(), 0}};\n      }),\n      std::array{\n          CTxOut{47 * COIN, spk2},\n      },\n  };\n  ```\n\n- **Complexity:** a constrained template versus a plain C++11 constructor. \n\nLet me know if I've missed or misrepresented anything.",
